It is unnecessary, because child constructor automatically call public parent constructor without any parameters
```
#include<iostream>

using namespace std;

class basic {
  public: basic() {
    cout << "Basic\n";
  }
};
class child1: public basic {
  public: child1() {
    cout << "Child1\n";
  }
};
class child2: public basic {
  public: child2(): basic() {
    cout << "Child2\n";
  }
};

int main() {
  child1 c1;
  child2 c2;
}
```
This code prints same output for each object
```
Basic
Child1
Basic
Child2
```

That looks great!

For the `CollisionPolygon2D` case, apart from the change I've mentioned, I'd like to actually refactor it a little bit since you're updating collision debug draw.

It doesn't make a lot of sense for polygon shapes to be displayed in a different color as other shapes. So here's my suggestion to make things more consistent:
```
			Color color = get_tree()->get_debug_collisions_color();

			draw_polyline(polygon, color);
			// Draw the last segment as it's not drawn by `canvas_item_add_polyline()`.
			draw_line(polygon[polygon.size() - 1], polygon[0], color);

#define DEBUG_DECOMPOSE
#if defined(TOOLS_ENABLED) && defined(DEBUG_DECOMPOSE)
			Vector<Vector<Vector2> > decomp = _decompose_in_convex();

			Color c = color;
			float min_value = 0.2;
			for (int i = 0; i < decomp.size(); i++) {
				draw_colored_polygon(decomp[i], c);
				float new_value = Math::fmod(c.get_v() + 0.738, 1.0);
				if (new_value < min_value) {
					new_value += min_value;
				}
				c.set_hsv(c.get_h(), c.get_s(), new_value, c.a);
			}
#else
			draw_colored_polygon(polygon, color);
#endif
```

It also alternates values instead of hues when drawing the different convex shapes from decomposition, which is colorblind-friendly and easier to read because it matches the style better.

